      "decline_reason": "Two attempts, two objections. The first added a transcript-reference gate that proved unbypassable by path tricks, but an attacker could ingest a session linking the credential file and then preview it — demonstrated live, HTTP 200 with credential contents. The second kept that gate and added an unconditional credential denylist, which held against every spelling, encoding, case and symlink variant tried — but its new mention-extraction regex is quadratic, handing the same unauthenticated attacker a CPU-exhaustion primitive that did not exist before. The security direction is right and most of the work is sound; it needs the parser made linear before it can ship.",
      "recommendation": "Restrict the preview to paths actually referenced by the requested session's transcript, or at minimum confine reads to the <flavor>/brain/** subtree and deny known credential filenames (oauth_creds.json, google_accounts.json, settings.json, .env, anything dot-prefixed at the root). From the attempts: keep both layers, but extract the `@[name] is a [File]:` mention by locating the literal \"] is a [File]:\" with indexOf and walking back to the preceding \"@[\" (strictly linear) rather than with a `[^\\]]*` regex; a possessive quantifier alone was measured still quadratic. Also worth doing, since the denylist only covers four names: confine reads to the <flavor>/brain/** subtree, because a non-denylisted secret such as ~/.gemini/mcp-oauth-tokens.json was still readable by the plant-then-preview path."
    }
  ]
}

