      "id": "F8",
      "title": "Cross-site GET triggers LLM-spending mining run; no Origin/Referer/CSRF check on any endpoint",
      "status": "declined",
      "claims": {
        "behaviour_unchanged": { "state": "NOT_CONFIDENT", "evidence": "with ALLOWED_HOSTS unset — the state of every existing deployment after applying the patch — curl against a reverse-proxied hostname and `agent-ingest --server http://0.0.0.0:8097` both return 403 where the patch base returns 200, and the README carries no upgrade note for the operators this breaks" }
      },
      "decline_reason": "Two attempts, two objections, and the security work itself is sound — the second attempt was confirmed with a real browser on the wire to block every cross-site shape (img, fetch, XHR, iframe, form POST, link navigation) including the attacker-published loopback-name bypass that sank the first attempt, while leaving curl, the Go CLI, the Playwright fixture and the no-Host compose healthcheck working, and it survived 27 raw-socket Host-parsing tricks. What blocks it is the rollout, not the mechanism: the fail-closed Host allow-list turns away legitimate callers on every non-loopback deployment, and the README documents the new ALLOWED_HOSTS variable for fresh setups without telling an existing operator to set it before upgrading — the symptom being that every /api route 403s and the UI goes blank. The patch also tells LAN/hostname operators the allow-list stops CSRF for them, which the verifier disproved on the wire. Both are fixable in documentation, but they are the user's call rather than something to ship silently.",
      "recommendation": "Reject browser-initiated cross-site requests: validate the Origin/Referer header (or require a custom header that a simple cross-origin request cannot set) for every /api route, and validate the Host header against an allow-list to defeat DNS rebinding. From the attempts: the working design is a purely additive @ServerFilter(\"/api/**\") that checks Host against an allow-list first (defaulting to localhost/127.0.0.1/::1 on any port, extendable by env var), then Sec-Fetch-Site when present, then Origin host+port against Host, then Referer, and allows a request carrying none of those markers so non-browser callers keep working. Two things to add before shipping it: an explicit upgrade callout naming the symptom and the ALLOWED_HOSTS remedy, and honest wording that for a configured plain-HTTP hostname it is API_TOKEN or HTTPS — not the allow-list — that closes CSRF, because browsers attach no fetch metadata to such an origin."
    }
  ]
}

